label Day4
# story entry day=4
# art scene draft.reading.late
\In the rehearsal room next door, a practice piano plays a slow four-bar phrase, pauses to correct a chord, and begins the same four notes again. #id:line.Day4.one
\Outside Studio B, the heavy door is ajar by less than an inch. Over the piano next door, two voices are talking near the wooden bench—unhurried, mid-conversation. #id:line.Day4.002
\“...you've made the chin too square.” #id:line.Day4.003
\“Then stop moving your head when you talk.” #id:line.Day4.004
\“I wasn't talking. I was breathing.” #id:line.Day4.005
\“Do less of that for thirty seconds.” #id:line.Day4.006
\A short, genuine laugh from the floorboards—Lavinia's—followed by the dry rustle of heavy paper closing. #id:line.Day4.007
\“I'll bring the rest on Friday,” the other voice says quietly. #id:line.Day4.008
\When the main door swings inward under the hand a moment later, the far side door by the dressing passage is just settling into its frame with a soft click. #id:line.Day4.009
\On the narrow wooden ledge beside the bench stands a low glass jar of water. White flowers sit inside it, their heads just above the rim. #id:line.Day4.010
\Lavinia sits on the rosin-warmed floorboards with her back resting against the front of the low wooden bench, both legs stretched out loosely in front of her. #id:line.Day4.011
\When the space on the bench behind her is taken, she leans the back of her head directly against my knee and reaches up for the paper cup. #id:line.Day4.012
\Her fingers close around the warm cardboard. #id:line.Day4.013
\“That isn't iced.” #id:line.Day4.014
\“The ice machine in the canteen was being cleaned.” #id:line.Day4.015
\“In October.” #id:line.Day4.016
\“They clean it on Thursdays.” #id:line.Day4.017
\“You're a terrible liar when you don't need to be.” #id:line.Day4.018
\“You're drinking it.” #id:line.Day4.019
\“Because my hands are cold.” She takes a slow mouthful over the rim, her head still resting against the wool of my skirt, and glances sideways at the open canvas bag beside my shoe. A paperback protrudes from the top, a strip of paper marking the middle pages. #id:line.Day4.020
\Without asking, her free hand reaches into the bag, draws the book out, and opens it to the marker. #id:line.Day4.021
\“Page eighty,” she says, tilting the leaf toward the window light. “Read the part you underlined twice.” #id:line.Day4.022
\“It's for tomorrow's seminar.” #id:line.Day4.023
\“You underlined it twice because you were pleased with your own point. Start from the top.” She settles the back of her neck more comfortably against my knee and watches my reflection in the wall-length mirror across the floorboards. #id:line.Day4.024
\Looking down at the page over her shoulder, I begin reading. #id:line.Day4.025
\By the fourth sentence, the footnote at the bottom of the page is clearly wrong about the heroine's motive, and it takes three more sentences to explain why the author ruined the chapter by making her apologize too soon. #id:line.Day4.026
\In the mirror across the room, Lavinia is no longer looking at the paperback; she is watching my mouth. #id:line.Day4.027
\When I pause at the margin note, she reaches up without turning her head and takes my free hand, drawing my palm down until it rests against her collarbone above the wool cardigan. #id:line.Day4.028
\“You still drop the end of the sentence,” she murmurs. #id:line.Day4.029
\“You still lean back to hear it.” #id:line.Day4.030
\“Before I went, I leaned in.” #id:line.Day4.031
\“And now?” #id:line.Day4.032
\Her thumb passes once across the knuckle of my index finger. #id:line.Day4.033
\“Keep reading,” she says softly. #id:line.Day4.034
\Her hand over mine. Not a word about Friday. #id:line.Day4.035
\“It's warm in here,” Lavinia says, releasing my hand only to slip her wool cardigan off her shoulders. #id:line.Day4.036
\As she shifts sideways to lay the cardigan on the bench, the late-afternoon light catches a dark shadow along the pale rib-seam of her practice bodice just above the left hip. #id:line.Day4.037
\At first glance it looks like a streak of grey dust from the window sill or the underside of the bench. #id:line.Day4.038
\Then she leans half an inch toward the mirror, and the shadow separates into four distinct marks. #id:line.Day4.039
\A wider oval pressed into the wool in front; three narrower lines curving back toward the spine. #id:line.Day4.040
\A left hand. #id:line.Day4.041
\And set two inches higher on the ribcage than any dance partner places his hand for a lift—not at the hip bone where weight is taken, but high on the ribs, where someone standing directly in front of the bench would take a sitter's waist in her left hand to turn the shoulder toward the window light. #id:line.Day4.042
\Before Lavinia can lean her head back against my knee, a folded linen handkerchief is already between my fingers, dampened against the warm condensation on the cup lid. #id:line.Day4.043
\“Hold still,” I say. #id:line.Day4.044
\“What are you doing?” #id:line.Day4.045
\The damp linen presses against the front mark, expecting dry dust to lift off the wool. #id:line.Day4.046
\It does not lift. Under the wet cloth, the grey mark smears darker—velvety, heavy, and slightly greasy against the pale weave. #id:line.Day4.047
\Only one person in this building uses soft, unlacquered 4B woodless graphite sticks that coat the heel of the left palm and the inside of the fingers because she dislikes the noise of a pencil sharpener in quiet rooms. Yesterday afternoon in the exhibition room, that exact grey film sat along the side of her thumb when she handed me half a mandarin; twenty minutes ago on these floorboards, she didn't even wipe her left hand on her rag before putting her fingers around Lavinia's waist. #id:line.Day4.048
\She held her ribs with the lead still on her skin. #id:line.Day4.049
\Beneath the thin bodice, the muscle over Lavinia's ribs flinches once, then goes taut under the sudden force of my hand. #id:line.Day4.050
\“There's pencil lead on your waist,” I say, my voice dropping half a tone. #id:line.Day4.051
\“She was turning my shoulder toward the window.” #id:line.Day4.052
\“With her bare hand on your ribs?” #id:line.Day4.053
\“I kept moving when I breathed.” In the mirror across the floorboards, Lavinia watches the muscle in my jaw tighten—and the corner of her mouth lifts in a slow, deliberate provocation. “Her hands were warm. Unlike yours.” #id:line.Day4.054
\The second pass of the damp linen scrubs hard enough to pull the seam of the bodice sideways against her skin. Because the 4B lead is soft and wax-heavy, the wet cloth only drives a faint grey shadow deeper into the pale wool. #id:line.Day4.055
\Dropping the stained handkerchief onto the bench, I slip my bare right hand directly over the damp waistband of her bodice—pressing my palm flat and hard against her ribs, thumb over the front smudge, fingers spanning the three marks toward her spine, holding her waist still against my knee until my own heat goes all the way through the damp wool into her skin. #id:line.Day4.056
\Above her collar, Lavinia's breath catches audibly. #id:line.Day4.057
\“That's warm enough,” she whispers. #id:line.Day4.058
\“It was still damp,” I say quietly, not taking my palm off her waist until her ribs rise twice against my hand. #id:line.Day4.059
\Slowly, she pulls herself up from the floorboards and sits on the wooden bench beside me, leaving me kneeling on the boards by her feet. Her eyes in the mirror are wide and bright. #id:line.Day4.060
\She reaches into her cardigan pocket, takes out a small tin of honey throat pastilles, and flicks the lid open with her thumb. #id:line.Day4.061
\One amber square slips over the metal rim, strikes the pleat of her wrap skirt just above the knee, and stays lodged in the hollow of the wool. #id:line.Day4.062
\Neither of us reaches for it with our fingers. #id:line.Day4.063
\“You forgot lunch again, didn't you?” she says, her voice still slightly breathless. #id:line.Day4.064
\“I had a biscuit.” #id:line.Day4.065
\“That isn't lunch.” In the mirror behind her back, her reflected eyes stay locked on the crown of my head. “Go on, then.” #id:line.Day4.066
# self-talk
\Take it from her skirt without using the hands. #id:line.Day4.067
label Day4.thought
# story marker {"kind":"thought.pass","payload":{"opportunity_id":"Day4.pastille","yes_target_id":"target.Day4.pastille.yes","pass_target_id":"target.Day4.pastille.default"}}
return

label Day4.pastille.yes
# story entry day=4
# art scene draft.reading.late
\Both knees stay on the floorboards between the edge of the bench and her shoes. #id:line.Day4.pastille.yes.001
\Lavinia's fingers stop on the rim of the open tin. #id:line.Day4.pastille.yes.002
\Instead of lifting my hand from the bench, my chin lowers across the wool of the wrap skirt. Beneath the cloth, the tendon at her knee pulls taut for half a breath. #id:line.Day4.pastille.yes.003
\My lips brush the fold of the dark wool and take the amber square cleanly. Lemon and sharp honey. #id:line.Day4.pastille.yes.004
\Before my head can rise, Lavinia's free hand comes down—fingers tangling into the hair at the nape of my neck, holding my face an inch above her knee. #id:line.Day4.pastille.yes.005
\“Nu te saturi niciodată,” she murmurs in Romanian. #id:line.Day4.pastille.yes.006
\Then, in English, her thumb grazing my right cheekbone\: #id:line.Day4.pastille.yes.007
\“All it takes is four marks of pencil on my waist.” #id:line.Day4.pastille.yes.008
\“They're gone now.” #id:line.Day4.pastille.yes.009
\“The grey is still on your handkerchief.” #id:line.Day4.pastille.yes.010
label Day4.pastille.yes.next
# story marker {"kind":"scene.transition","payload":{"target_id":"target.Day4.impulse"}}
return

label Day4.pastille.default
# story entry day=4
# art scene draft.reading.late
\Two fingers lift the amber square neatly from the pleat of the skirt and set it on the lid of her tin. #id:line.Day4.pastille.default.001
\“It didn't touch the floor.” #id:line.Day4.pastille.default.002
\Lavinia looks at the pastille on the metal lid, then down at my bare hand still resting near her hip. #id:line.Day4.pastille.default.003
\“Ce păcat,” she says under her breath. “You weren't so polite when you had your hand on my ribs.” #id:line.Day4.pastille.default.004
